# Queries as values: a SELECT, an INSERT, an UPDATE or a DELETE built by
# functions and composed as any value is, then written by `to_sql` as the
# text and the parameters a driver of Rebel runs, with each database's own
# placeholders: `$1` for PostgreSQL, `?1` for SQLite.
#
# It talks to no database and depends on no driver: `to_sql` answers a
# `Statement`, whose `text` and `params` go to `exec` or `query` of
# `sql/postgres` or `sql/sqlite` as they are. Reading the rows back is
# `sql`'s (`sql.text_named` and the rest), and a record's own field names,
# `User::name` (spec § 9), name the columns on both sides, so the compiler
# checks them. It makes no table (a migration's SQL does, `sql/migrate`),
# and it is no ORM: no object is tracked, saved or loaded behind a program,
# for that needs reflection and objects shared between places, and here
# every value is a copy.
#
# **A value is a parameter, always.** No function takes a value into the
# text: a value goes in as a `sql.Value` and comes out in `params`, so no
# value can change what a statement means. Every name is quoted, always, by
# `sql.quote_identifier`. A name given as text is cut at its dots into its
# parts: `users.id` is the column `id` of the table `users`. The one way to
# write SQL this package does not offer is `raw_without_values`, whose text
# may hold no value and is refused where it holds a quote, a semicolon, a
# comment, a `?` or a `$`.
#
# A query is checked whole when `to_sql` writes it, and a mistake fails
# there, never in the database: `invalid` for a name that is empty or holds
# a zero byte, a row of the wrong length, an UPDATE or a DELETE with no
# condition, a `NULL` where no comparison can match it, and a fragment, an
# operator or a type that could carry a value; `unsupported` for what
# PostgreSQL writes and SQLite does not; and `limit` for more parameters than
# PostgreSQL's protocol carries.
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: PostgreSQL's DISTINCT
# ON; a value compared with ANY or ALL of an array or a subquery; window
# functions: OVER, PARTITION BY, frames; a text compared by a named collation; a
# subquery that reads the tables before it, LATERAL; named subqueries before the
# statement, WITH and WITH RECURSIVE; rows locked as they are read: FOR UPDATE,
# FOR SHARE, SKIP LOCKED, NOWAIT; rows changed by what another table holds,
# UPDATE ... FROM; rows deleted by what another table holds, DELETE ... USING;
# MySQL's and MariaDB's text: backquoted names, bare `?`.

use sql
use sql/build/internal/clauses
use sql/build/internal/statements
use sql/build/internal/tokens

## Codes

# The code of a query `to_sql` refuses: a name that is empty or holds a zero
# byte, a row of the wrong length, an UPDATE or a DELETE with no condition, a
# `NULL` where no comparison can match it, a fragment, an operator or a type
# that could carry a value. Every maker of a fragment and `to_sql` fail with
# it.
constant INVALID: str
    "invalid"

# The code of what one dialect writes and the one asked does not: `to_sql`.
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    "unsupported"

# The code of a statement with more parameters than PostgreSQL's protocol
# carries, 65,535: `to_sql`.
constant LIMIT: str
    "limit"

## Databases

# The database a statement is written for: the placeholders it reads and
# what it supports.
variant Dialect
    # PostgreSQL: placeholders `$1`, `$2` and on.
    postgres
    # SQLite: placeholders `?1`, `?2` and on.
    sqlite

# A statement as a driver takes it: `postgres.query(@db, s.text, s.params)`.
record Statement
    # The statement's text, every value in it a placeholder.
    text: str
    # The values of the placeholders, in order: the first is `$1` or `?1`.
    params: [sql.Value]

## Expressions

# A value a statement computes: a column, a parameter, a function's call,
# an operation. Only the functions under this heading and `subquery` make
# one. Its one field is its private pieces, which only this package reads.
record Expr
    # The pieces of its text and what kind of expression it is.
    part: clauses.Part

# The column `name`: `"name"`, or `"users"."id"` for `users.id`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots into its parts, the table's
#   (and the schema's) before the column's; `User::name` names a record's
#   field (spec § 9).
#
# An empty part, as in `users.` or "", fails `to_sql` `invalid`. See also
# `columns_named`, `every_column`.
function column(name: str) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.column_part(name))

# The columns `names`, in order, as `column` makes each one.
#
# - `names`: the columns' names, each cut at its dots; `[]` gives `[]`.
#
# The answer is as long as `names`. See also `column`.
function columns_named(names: [str]) -> [Expr]
    return names.map(column)

# Every column of every table a SELECT reads: `*`.
#
# See also `every_column_of`, `count_all`.
function every_column() -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.star_part())

# Every column of the table `name`: `"users".*`.
#
# - `name`: the table's name or its other name in the query, cut at its
#   dots.
#
# See also `every_column`.
function every_column_of(name: str) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.star_of_part(name))

# The value `v`, as a parameter: `$1` or `?1`, with `v` in the statement's
# `params`.
#
# - `v`: the value; `.null_value` is SQL's `NULL`, which `compare` and
#   `equal` write as `IS NULL`.
#
# See also `equal`.
function parameter(v: sql.Value) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.parameter_part(v))

# A call of the function of SQL `name` on `arguments`: `lower("name")`,
# `coalesce("nick", $1)`.
#
# - `name`: the function's name as the database knows it, written as it is:
#   letters, digits and `_`, a letter or `_` first, with dots between a
#   schema's name and the function's.
# - `arguments`: the arguments, in order; `[]` for none, `now()`.
#
# A name of any other form fails `to_sql` `invalid`, since it is written
# unquoted. See also `call_distinct`, `count_all`.
function call(name: str, arguments: [Expr]) -> Expr
    parts: [clauses.Part] @= []

    for a in arguments
        parts @ parts.push(a.part)

    return Expr(part: clauses.call_part(name, arguments: parts, distinct: false))

# A call of the aggregate `name` on the distinct values of `arguments` alone:
# `count(DISTINCT "city")`.
#
# - `name`: the aggregate's name, as `call` takes it.
# - `arguments`: the arguments, at least one.
#
# Fails `to_sql` `invalid` where `arguments` is empty, or the name as `call`
# says. See also `call`.
function call_distinct(name: str, arguments: [Expr]) -> Expr
    parts: [clauses.Part] @= []

    for a in arguments
        parts @ parts.push(a.part)

    return Expr(part: clauses.call_part(name, arguments: parts, distinct: true))

# The count of the rows: `count(*)`.
#
# See also `call`, `aliased`.
function count_all() -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.count_all_part())

# `e` read as the type `type_name`: `CAST($1 AS uuid)`.
#
# - `e`: the expression converted.
# - `type_name`: the type as the database writes it, `bigint`, `numeric(10,
#   2)`, `timestamp with time zone`, `int[]`: letters, digits, `_`, spaces,
#   dots, commas, parentheses and brackets, a letter or `_` first.
#
# A type of any other form fails `to_sql` `invalid`, since it is written as
# it is. See also `parameter`.
function cast(e: Expr, type_name: str) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.cast_part(e.part, type_name: type_name))

# `left` and `right` joined by `operator`: `"price" * "quantity"`, `"first"
# || $1`.
#
# - `left`: the operand before the operator, in parentheses where it is an
#   operation itself.
# - `operator`: the operator as the database writes it: either characters
#   of `+ - * / % < > = ~ ! @ # ^ & | ?` alone, or letters and spaces
#   alone (`COLLATE` is not one: it takes a name).
# - `right`: the operand after it, in parentheses where it is an operation.
#
# An operator of any other form, one holding `--` or `/*`, and `?` for
# SQLite, where it would be read as a placeholder, fail `to_sql` `invalid`.
# See also `compare`.
function operation(left: Expr, operator: str, right: Expr) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.operation_part(left: left.part, operator: operator, right: right.part))

# `e` under the other name `alias`: `count(*) AS "total"`. A row names the
# column so, which a record's field can name: `aliased(count_all(), alias:
# Stats::total)`.
#
# - `e`: the expression named.
# - `alias`: the name the column takes, quoted.
#
# Only a column of a SELECT or of a RETURNING may be aliased: one anywhere
# else fails `to_sql` `invalid`. See also `table_as`.
function aliased(e: Expr, alias: str) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.aliased_part(e.part, alias: alias))

# The value an INSERT proposed for the column `name` and could not insert, inside
# its `do_update`: `excluded."count"`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, one part.
#
# See also `Conflict`, `assign`.
function excluded(name: str) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.excluded_part(name))

# `query` as a value inside another statement, in parentheses: a column
# computed by a SELECT, or the operand of `in_query`.
#
# - `query`: the SELECT; it should answer one column, and one row where it
#   stands as a value, which the database checks.
#
# Its parameters are numbered with the statement's own. See also
# `in_query`, `exists`, `from_subquery`.
function subquery(query: Select) -> Expr
    columns: [clauses.Part] @= []
    tables: [[tokens.Token]] @= []
    joins: [[tokens.Token]] @= []
    where: [clauses.Clause] @= []
    group_by: [clauses.Part] @= []
    having: [clauses.Clause] @= []
    order_by: [[tokens.Token]] @= []
    combined: [[tokens.Token]] @= []

    for e in query.columns
        columns @ columns.push(e.part)

    for t in query.from
        tables @ tables.push(t.pieces)

    for j in query.joins
        on: [clauses.Clause] @= []

        for c in j.on
            on @ on.push(c.clause)

        keyword = match j.kind
            .inner => "INNER JOIN"
            .left  => "LEFT JOIN"
            .right => "RIGHT JOIN"
            .full  => "FULL JOIN"
            .cross => "CROSS JOIN"

        joins @ joins.push(
            statements.join_piece(keyword: keyword, table: j.table.pieces, on: on, cross: j.kind == .cross)
        )

    for c in query.where
        where @ where.push(c.clause)

    for e in query.group_by
        group_by @ group_by.push(e.part)

    for c in query.having
        having @ having.push(c.clause)

    for o in query.order_by
        order_by @ order_by.push(
            statements.order_piece(o.by.part, descending: o.descending, nulls_first: o.nulls == .first, nulls_last: o.nulls == .last)
        )

    for c in query.combined
        keyword = match c.operation
            .union_distinct     => "UNION"
            .union_all          => "UNION ALL"
            .intersect_distinct => "INTERSECT"
            .intersect_all      => "INTERSECT ALL"
            .except_distinct    => "EXCEPT"
            .except_all         => "EXCEPT ALL"

        whole = subquery(c.query).part.pieces
        own = c.query.order_by.len() > 0 || !c.query.limit.is_err() || !c.query.offset.is_err()
        postgres_only = c.operation == .intersect_all || c.operation == .except_all
        combined @ combined.push(
            statements.combined_piece(keyword: keyword, select: whole.slice(from: 1, to: whole.len() - 1), postgres_only: postgres_only, own_order: own)
        )

    parts = statements.SelectParts(
        distinct: query.distinct,
        columns: columns,
        from: tables,
        joins: joins,
        where: where,
        group_by: group_by,
        having: having,
        order_by: order_by,
        limit: query.limit,
        offset: query.offset,
        combined: combined
    )

    return Expr(part: clauses.subquery_part(statements.select_pieces(parts)))

# What a `choose` answers where `condition` holds.
record When
    # The condition asked, in the order written: the first that holds
    # chooses.
    condition: Condition
    # The value answered where it holds.
    result: Expr

# The `result` of the first of `whens` whose condition holds, `otherwise`
# where none does: `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END`.
#
# - `whens`: the conditions and their results, at least one.
# - `otherwise`: the value where no condition holds; `parameter(.null_value)`
#   for `NULL`.
#
# Fails `to_sql` `invalid` where `whens` is empty. See also `When`.
function choose(whens: [When], otherwise: Expr) -> Expr
    conditions: [clauses.Clause] @= []
    results: [clauses.Part] @= []

    for w in whens
        conditions @ conditions.push(w.condition.clause)
        results @ results.push(w.result.part)

    return Expr(part: clauses.chosen_part(conditions, results: results, otherwise: otherwise.part))

# A fragment of SQL written as it is, for what this package does not offer:
# `raw_without_values("CURRENT_DATE")`. **It must hold no value**: a value
# goes in a parameter, through `parameter`.
#
# - `text`: the fragment. It may not be empty, and may not hold a `'` (a
#   text's quote), a `;` (a second statement), `--` or `/*` (a comment), a
#   `?` or a `$` (a placeholder, which would break the numbering), or a zero
#   byte.
#
# A fragment that does fails `to_sql` `invalid`, saying which. It is put in
# parentheses where it is an operand. See also `holds`, `parameter`.
function raw_without_values(text: str) -> Expr
    return Expr(part: clauses.raw_part(text))

## Conditions

# A condition a row holds or not: built by the functions under this heading
# and combined by `all_of`, `any_of` and `negate`. Its one field is its
# private pieces, which only this package reads.
record Condition
    # The pieces of its text and how it is joined.
    clause: clauses.Clause

# How `compare` compares.
variant Op
    # `=`; `IS NULL` where one side is the value `NULL`.
    equal
    # `<>`; `IS NOT NULL` where one side is the value `NULL`.
    not_equal
    # `<`.
    less
    # `<=`.
    less_or_equal
    # `>`.
    greater
    # `>=`.
    greater_or_equal
    # `LIKE`, the right side a pattern, its escape a backslash.
    like
    # Another operator of the database, as `operation` takes it: `ILIKE`,
    # `~`, `IS DISTINCT FROM`, `@>`.
    other
        # The operator, checked as `operation` checks it.
        operator: str

# Whether the column `name` holds `value`: `"name" = $1`, or `"left_at" IS NULL`
# where `value` is `NULL`, since `= NULL` holds for no row.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `value`: the value compared, a parameter.
#
# See also `not_equal`, `compare`, `in_values`.
function equal(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .equal, right: clauses.parameter_part(value))
    )

# Whether the column `name` holds another value than `value`: `"name" <> $1`, or
# `"left_at" IS NOT NULL` where `value` is `NULL`. A row whose column is
# `NULL` holds neither `equal` nor this.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `value`: the value compared, a parameter.
#
# See also `equal`.
function not_equal(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .not_equal, right: clauses.parameter_part(value))
    )

# Whether the column `name` holds less than `value`: `"age" < $1`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `value`: the bound, a parameter; `NULL` fails `to_sql` `invalid`, since
#   nothing is less than it.
#
# See also `less_or_equal`, `between`.
function less(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .ordered(symbol: "<"), right: clauses.parameter_part(value))
    )

# Whether the column `name` holds at most `value`: `"age" <= $1`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `value`: the bound, a parameter; `NULL` fails `to_sql` `invalid`.
#
# See also `less`.
function less_or_equal(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .ordered(symbol: "<="), right: clauses.parameter_part(value))
    )

# Whether the column `name` holds more than `value`: `"age" > $1`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `value`: the bound, a parameter; `NULL` fails `to_sql` `invalid`.
#
# See also `greater_or_equal`.
function greater(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .ordered(symbol: ">"), right: clauses.parameter_part(value))
    )

# Whether the column `name` holds at least `value`: `"age" >= $1`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `value`: the bound, a parameter; `NULL` fails `to_sql` `invalid`.
#
# See also `greater`.
function greater_or_equal(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .ordered(symbol: ">="), right: clauses.parameter_part(value))
    )

# Whether the text in the column `name` matches `pattern`: `"name" LIKE $1 ESCAPE
# $2`, where `%` matches any text and `_` any one character.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `pattern`: the pattern, a parameter; a backslash before `%`, `_` or a
#   backslash matches that character itself, and `escape_like` writes a
#   text so.
#
# The backslash is named as the escape in a second parameter, so a pattern
# means the same in both databases. PostgreSQL compares case for case;
# SQLite ignores the case of ASCII letters. See also `escape_like`,
# `compare`.
function like(name: str, pattern: str) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(name), how: .like, right: clauses.parameter_part(.text(v: pattern)))
    )

# `text` with a backslash before each `%`, `_` and backslash, so that
# `like` matches it as itself: `escape_like(s) + "%"` matches what begins
# with `s`.
#
# - `text`: the text to match as it is; "" gives "".
#
# See also `like`.
function escape_like(text: str) -> str
    pieces: [str] @= []

    for c in text.chars()
        if c == "%" || c == "_" || c == "\\"
            pieces @ pieces.push("\\")
        pieces @ pieces.push(c)

    return pieces.join("")

# Whether the column `name` holds one of `values`: `"id" IN ($1, $2, $3)`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `values`: the values, each a parameter. An empty list holds for no row,
#   written `1 = 0`, since PostgreSQL reads no `IN ()`. A `NULL` among them
#   fails `to_sql` `invalid`: it matches no row, and under `negate` it would
#   make the condition hold for none.
#
# See also `in_query`, `equal`.
function in_values(name: str, values: [sql.Value]) -> Condition
    return Condition(clause: clauses.in_values_clause(clauses.column_part(name), values: values))

# Whether the column `name` holds a value from `low` to `high`, both included:
# `"age" BETWEEN $1 AND $2`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `low`: the smallest value that holds, a parameter.
# - `high`: the largest value that holds, a parameter; where it is below
#   `low`, no row holds.
#
# A `NULL` bound fails `to_sql` `invalid`. See also `less`, `greater`.
function between(name: str, low: sql.Value, high: sql.Value) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.between_clause(subject: clauses.column_part(name), low: clauses.parameter_part(low), high: clauses.parameter_part(high))
    )

# Whether two columns hold the same value: `"users"."id" =
# "posts"."user_id"`, as a join's condition is.
#
# - `left`: the first column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `right`: the second column's name, cut at its dots.
#
# A row where either is `NULL` does not hold it. See also `inner_join`,
# `compare`.
function columns_equal(left: str, right: str) -> Condition
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.compared(left: clauses.column_part(left), how: .equal, right: clauses.column_part(right))
    )

# Whether `left` and `right` compare by `op`: the general form of `equal`
# and the rest, for any two expressions.
#
# - `left`: the expression before the operator.
# - `op`: how they compare; `.equal` and `.not_equal` write `IS NULL` and
#   `IS NOT NULL` where one side is `parameter(.null_value)`.
# - `right`: the expression after it.
#
# A `NULL` value beside any other `op` fails `to_sql` `invalid`, and an
# operator `.other` names as `operation` says. See also `equal`,
# `operation`.
function compare(left: Expr, op: Op, right: Expr) -> Condition
    how: clauses.Comparison = match op
        .equal            => .equal
        .not_equal        => .not_equal
        .less             => .ordered(symbol: "<")
        .less_or_equal    => .ordered(symbol: "<=")
        .greater          => .ordered(symbol: ">")
        .greater_or_equal => .ordered(symbol: ">=")
        .like             => .like
        .other o          => .other(operator: o.operator)

    return Condition(clause: clauses.compared(left: left.part, how: how, right: right.part))

# Whether the column `name` holds a value among the rows `query` answers: `"id" IN
# (SELECT ...)`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
# - `query`: a SELECT of one column.
#
# See also `in_values`, `exists`.
function in_query(name: str, query: Select) -> Condition
    whole = subquery(query).part.pieces
    return Condition(
        clause: clauses.in_query_clause(clauses.column_part(name), select: whole.slice(from: 1, to: whole.len() - 1))
    )

# Whether `query` answers at least one row: `EXISTS (SELECT ...)`.
#
# - `query`: the SELECT; its columns do not matter.
#
# See also `in_query`, `negate`.
function exists(query: Select) -> Condition
    whole = subquery(query).part.pieces
    return Condition(clause: clauses.exists_clause(whole.slice(from: 1, to: whole.len() - 1)))

# Whether every one of `conditions` holds: each in parentheses where it is
# an `any_of`, joined by `AND`.
#
# - `conditions`: the conditions, in order. An empty list holds for every
#   row, written `1 = 1`; one condition is itself.
#
# See also `any_of`, `negate`.
function all_of(conditions: [Condition]) -> Condition
    parts: [clauses.Clause] @= []

    for c in conditions
        parts @ parts.push(c.clause)

    return Condition(clause: clauses.all_clause(parts))

# Whether at least one of `conditions` holds: each in parentheses where it
# is an `all_of`, joined by `OR`.
#
# - `conditions`: the conditions, in order. An empty list holds for no row,
#   written `1 = 0`; one condition is itself.
#
# See also `all_of`.
function any_of(conditions: [Condition]) -> Condition
    parts: [clauses.Clause] @= []

    for c in conditions
        parts @ parts.push(c.clause)

    return Condition(clause: clauses.any_clause(parts))

# Whether `condition` does not hold: `NOT (...)`. A row for which it is
# unknown, because of a `NULL`, holds neither it nor this.
#
# - `condition`: the condition denied.
#
# See also `all_of`, `any_of`.
function negate(condition: Condition) -> Condition
    return Condition(clause: clauses.negated(condition.clause))

# The condition that `e` is true: a column of booleans, or a fragment
# `raw_without_values` writes.
#
# - `e`: an expression the database reads as a boolean; it checks that it
#   is one.
#
# See also `raw_without_values`.
function holds(e: Expr) -> Condition
    return Condition(clause: clauses.holds_clause(e.part))

# The condition every row holds, `1 = 1`, which an UPDATE or a DELETE meant
# for every row of its table names in its `where`: one with an empty
# `where` fails `to_sql` `invalid`.
#
# See also `update`, `delete`.
function every_row() -> Condition
    return Condition(clause: clauses.all_clause([]))

## Tables and joins

# A table a statement reads, under its own name or another, or a subquery
# read as one. Only `table`, `table_as` and `from_subquery` make one. Its
# field is its private pieces.
record Table
    # The pieces of its text.
    pieces: [tokens.Token]

# The table `name`: `"users"`, `"app"."users"`.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots, a schema's before it.
#
# See also `table_as`, `select`.
function table(name: str) -> Table
    return Table(pieces: tokens.named(name))

# The table `name` under the other name `alias`, which the query's columns
# name it by: `"users" AS "u"`.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `alias`: its name in the query, one part.
#
# See also `table`, `from_subquery`.
function table_as(name: str, alias: str) -> Table
    return Table(pieces: tokens.concat(first: tokens.named(name), second: [.word(text: " AS "), .name(parts: [alias])]))

# The rows of `query` read as a table named `alias`: `(SELECT ...) AS
# "recent"`.
#
# - `query`: the SELECT.
# - `alias`: the name its columns are read under; both databases need one.
#
# See also `subquery`, `table_as`.
function from_subquery(query: Select, alias: str) -> Table
    return Table(
        pieces: tokens.concat(first: subquery(query).part.pieces, second: [.word(text: " AS "), .name(parts: [alias])])
    )

# How a join keeps rows that find no match.
variant JoinKind
    # `INNER JOIN`: only the rows that match.
    inner
    # `LEFT JOIN`: every row of the tables before it, the joined table's
    # columns `NULL` where none matches.
    left
    # `RIGHT JOIN`: every row of the joined table; SQLite reads it from
    # 3.39.
    right
    # `FULL JOIN`: every row of both sides; SQLite reads it from 3.39.
    full
    # `CROSS JOIN`: every row with every row, and no condition.
    cross

# A table joined to the ones a SELECT reads before it.
record Join
    # How rows with no match are kept.
    kind: JoinKind
    # The table joined.
    table: Table
    # The conditions a pair of rows holds, joined by `AND`: at least one,
    # but none for a `cross` join.
    on: [Condition]

# The table `name` names, joined where `on` holds, keeping only the rows
# that match.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots; `Join` built whole takes
#   `table_as` for another name.
# - `on`: the condition; `columns_equal` for the usual one.
#
# See also `left_join`, `cross_join`, `Join`.
function inner_join(name: str, on: Condition) -> Join
    return Join(kind: .inner, table: table(name), on: [on])

# The table `name` names, joined where `on` holds, keeping every row
# before it.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `on`: the condition.
#
# See also `inner_join`.
function left_join(name: str, on: Condition) -> Join
    return Join(kind: .left, table: table(name), on: [on])

# The table `name` names, joined where `on` holds, keeping every row of
# that table.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `on`: the condition.
#
# See also `left_join`.
function right_join(name: str, on: Condition) -> Join
    return Join(kind: .right, table: table(name), on: [on])

# The table `name` names, joined where `on` holds, keeping every row of
# both sides.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `on`: the condition.
#
# See also `left_join`, `right_join`.
function full_join(name: str, on: Condition) -> Join
    return Join(kind: .full, table: table(name), on: [on])

# The table `name` names, joined with no condition: each row with each of
# its rows.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
#
# See also `inner_join`.
function cross_join(name: str) -> Join
    return Join(kind: .cross, table: table(name), on: [])

## Order

# Where `NULL` goes in an order.
variant NullsAt
    # Where the database puts it: last going up and first going down for
    # PostgreSQL, first going up for SQLite.
    database_default
    # Before every value: `NULLS FIRST`.
    first
    # After every value: `NULLS LAST`.
    last

# One key of a SELECT's order.
record Order
    # What is ordered by.
    by: Expr
    # Whether from the largest down: `DESC`.
    descending: bool
    # Where `NULL` goes.
    nulls: NullsAt

# The order by the column `name` from the smallest up, `NULL` where the database
# puts it.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
#
# See also `descending`, `Order`.
function ascending(name: str) -> Order
    return Order(by: column(name), descending: false, nulls: .database_default)

# The order by the column `name` from the largest down, `NULL` where the database
# puts it.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, cut at its dots.
#
# See also `ascending`.
function descending(name: str) -> Order
    return Order(by: column(name), descending: true, nulls: .database_default)

## Statements

# How a SELECT's rows are combined with another's.
variant SetOperation
    # `UNION`: the rows of either, duplicates dropped.
    union_distinct
    # `UNION ALL`: the rows of either, every one.
    union_all
    # `INTERSECT`: the rows of both, duplicates dropped.
    intersect_distinct
    # `INTERSECT ALL`: PostgreSQL's alone.
    intersect_all
    # `EXCEPT`: the rows of the first that the second does not answer.
    except_distinct
    # `EXCEPT ALL`: PostgreSQL's alone.
    except_all

# A SELECT whose rows are combined with the rows before it.
record Combined
    # How they combine.
    operation: SetOperation
    # The SELECT, with no order, limit or offset of its own: the first
    # SELECT's apply to the whole.
    query: Select

# A SELECT. `select` makes one; a program sets the fields it needs with `@`
# and every other keeps its empty value, which writes nothing.
record Select
    # Whether duplicate rows are dropped: `SELECT DISTINCT`.
    distinct: bool
    # The columns answered, in order, at least one; `every_column` for `*`.
    columns: [Expr]
    # The tables read, `[]` for a SELECT of values alone.
    from: [Table]
    # The tables joined after them, in order.
    joins: [Join]
    # The conditions a row holds, joined by `AND`; `[]` for every row.
    where: [Condition]
    # What the rows are grouped by.
    group_by: [Expr]
    # The conditions a group holds, joined by `AND`.
    having: [Condition]
    # The order of the rows, the first key first.
    order_by: [Order]
    # At most so many rows, a parameter; `ok(10)` sets it, and a failure,
    # as `select` leaves it, sets none.
    limit: i64?
    # The rows skipped before the first answered, a parameter; a failure
    # for none.
    offset: i64?
    # The SELECTs combined with this one, in order.
    combined: [Combined]

# The SELECT of `columns` from the table `from`, every other part empty.
#
# - `from`: the table's name, cut at its dots; a program sets `from`
#   itself for another name, a subquery or none.
# - `columns`: the columns answered, at least one.
#
# Fails `to_sql` `invalid` where `columns` is empty. See also `Select`,
# `to_sql`.
function select(from: str, columns: [Expr]) -> Select
    return Select(
        distinct: false,
        columns: columns,
        from: [table(from)],
        joins: [],
        where: [],
        group_by: [],
        having: [],
        order_by: [],
        limit: fail(code: sql.NOT_FOUND, msg: "no limit"),
        offset: fail(code: sql.NOT_FOUND, msg: "no offset"),
        combined: []
    )

# What an INSERT inserts.
variant Source
    # Rows of values, each holding one value per column, in the columns'
    # order.
    rows
        # The rows, at least one.
        values: [[sql.Value]]
    # The rows a SELECT answers.
    query
        # The SELECT, answering one column per column inserted.
        select: Select
    # One row of the table's defaults, `DEFAULT VALUES`, with no column
    # named.
    defaults

# A column and the value an UPDATE, or an INSERT's `do_update`, gives it.
record Assignment
    # The column's name, one part.
    column: str
    # The value: `parameter` for a parameter, `excluded` for the value an INSERT
    # proposed, any expression.
    value: Expr

# The column `name` given `value`, a parameter: `"name" = $1`.
#
# - `name`: the column's name, one part.
# - `value`: its new value.
#
# See also `Assignment`, `update`.
function assign(name: str, value: sql.Value) -> Assignment
    return Assignment(column: name, value: parameter(value))

# What an INSERT does with a row a unique key already holds.
variant Conflict
    # Nothing written: the database refuses the row, and the driver fails
    # `exists`.
    unhandled
    # `ON CONFLICT (...) DO NOTHING`: the row is skipped.
    do_nothing
        # The columns of the unique key; `[]` for any key.
        target: [str]
    # `ON CONFLICT (...) DO UPDATE SET ...`: the row there is changed.
    do_update
        # The columns of the unique key, at least one.
        target: [str]
        # The changes, at least one; `excluded` reads the row proposed.
        set: [Assignment]

# An INSERT. `insert` and `insert_select` make one.
record Insert
    # The table's name, cut at its dots.
    into: str
    # The columns given, in order.
    columns: [str]
    # What is inserted.
    source: Source
    # What a row a unique key holds does.
    conflict: Conflict
    # The columns answered of each row inserted, `[]` for none; the driver's
    # `query` reads them.
    returning: [Expr]

# The INSERT of `rows` into `columns` of `into`.
#
# - `into`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `columns`: the columns, at least one.
# - `rows`: the rows, at least one, each one value per column.
#
# Fails `to_sql` `invalid` where `rows` is empty or a row's length is not
# `columns`'s, the message saying which row, counted from 0. See also
# `insert_select`, `Insert`.
function insert(into: str, columns: [str], rows: [[sql.Value]]) -> Insert
    return Insert(into: into, columns: columns, source: .rows(values: rows), conflict: .unhandled, returning: [])

# The INSERT of the rows `query` answers into `columns` of `into`.
#
# - `into`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `columns`: the columns, at least one, one per column `query` answers.
# - `query`: the SELECT.
#
# See also `insert`.
function insert_select(into: str, columns: [str], query: Select) -> Insert
    return Insert(into: into, columns: columns, source: .query(select: query), conflict: .unhandled, returning: [])

# An UPDATE. `update` makes one.
record Update
    # The table's name, cut at its dots.
    table: str
    # The changes, at least one.
    set: [Assignment]
    # The conditions a row changed holds, joined by `AND`, at least one:
    # `[every_row()]` for every row.
    where: [Condition]
    # The columns answered of each row changed, `[]` for none.
    returning: [Expr]

# The UPDATE of the table `name` that makes `set`'s changes, with no condition yet:
# a program sets `where` before `to_sql`, which refuses it empty.
#
# - `name`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
# - `set`: the changes, at least one.
#
# See also `assign`, `every_row`, `Update`.
function update(name: str, set: [Assignment]) -> Update
    return Update(table: name, set: set, where: [], returning: [])

# A DELETE. `delete` makes one.
record Delete
    # The table's name, cut at its dots.
    from: str
    # The conditions a row deleted holds, joined by `AND`, at least one:
    # `[every_row()]` for every row.
    where: [Condition]
    # The columns answered of each row deleted, `[]` for none.
    returning: [Expr]

# The DELETE from `from`, with no condition yet: a program sets `where`
# before `to_sql`, which refuses it empty.
#
# - `from`: the table's name, cut at its dots.
#
# See also `every_row`, `Delete`.
function delete(from: str) -> Delete
    return Delete(from: from, where: [], returning: [])

# A statement `to_sql` writes.
variant Query
    # A SELECT.
    select
        # The SELECT.
        query: Select
    # An INSERT.
    insert
        # The INSERT.
        query: Insert
    # An UPDATE.
    update
        # The UPDATE.
        query: Update
    # A DELETE.
    delete
        # The DELETE.
        query: Delete

## Writing

# `query` written for `dialect`: its text, every name quoted and every
# value a placeholder, and the values in order.
#
# - `query`: the statement.
# - `dialect`: the database: `$1` placeholders for `.postgres`, `?1` for
#   `.sqlite`.
#
# The same query writes the same bytes every time.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the query has a mistake (the module's comment), the
# message saying where; `unsupported` where it asks of SQLite what only
# PostgreSQL does (`INTERSECT ALL`, `EXCEPT ALL`); `limit` where it holds
# more than 65535 values for PostgreSQL. See also `Statement`.
function to_sql(query: Query, dialect: Dialect) -> Statement?
    pieces: [tokens.Token] = match query
        .select s =>
            whole = subquery(s.query).part.pieces
            whole.slice(from: 1, to: whole.len() - 1)
        .insert i =>
            q = i.query
            set: [[tokens.Token]] @= []
            returning: [clauses.Part] @= []
            defaults: bool @= false

            for e in q.returning
                returning @ returning.push(e.part)

            conflict: [tokens.Token] = match q.conflict
                .unhandled    => []
                .do_nothing d => statements.conflict_nothing(d.target)
                .do_update d =>
                    for a in d.set
                        set @ set.push(statements.assignment_piece(column: a.column, value: a.value.part))

                    statements.conflict_update(names: d.target, set: set)

            source: [tokens.Token] = match q.source
                .rows r => statements.rows_piece(width: q.columns.len(), rows: r.values)
                .query x =>
                    chosen @= x.select

                    if conflict.len() > 0 && chosen.where.len() == 0
                        chosen.where @ [every_row()]
                    whole = subquery(chosen).part.pieces
                    tokens.concat(first: [.word(text: " ")], second: whole.slice(from: 1, to: whole.len() - 1))
                .defaults =>
                    defaults @ true
                    []

            statements.insert_pieces(
                into: q.into,
                columns: q.columns,
                source: source,
                defaults: defaults,
                conflict: conflict,
                returning: returning
            )
        .update u =>
            q = u.query
            set: [[tokens.Token]] @= []
            where: [clauses.Clause] @= []
            returning: [clauses.Part] @= []

            for a in q.set
                set @ set.push(statements.assignment_piece(column: a.column, value: a.value.part))

            for c in q.where
                where @ where.push(c.clause)

            for e in q.returning
                returning @ returning.push(e.part)

            statements.update_pieces(table: q.table, set: set, where: where, returning: returning)
        .delete d =>
            q = d.query
            where: [clauses.Clause] @= []
            returning: [clauses.Part] @= []

            for c in q.where
                where @ where.push(c.clause)

            for e in q.returning
                returning @ returning.push(e.part)

            statements.delete_pieces(from: q.from, where: where, returning: returning)

    written = tokens.written(pieces, postgres: dialect == .postgres)?
    return ok(Statement(text: written.text, params: written.params))

## Tests

test "column and columns_named: one part, two, none, an empty part refused"
    q @= select(from: "app.users", columns: columns_named(["id", "users.name"]))
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\", \"users\".\"name\" FROM \"app\".\"users\""
    assert columns_named([]) == []
    q.columns @ [column("users.")]

    match to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == sql.INVALID && e.msg == "the name \"users.\": part 1 of the name is empty, and no database keeps an empty name"

    q.columns @ [column("")]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).is_err()

test "select: no column refused, a SELECT of values alone, a name of more than one byte"
    match to_sql(.select(query: select(from: "t", columns: [])), .postgres)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == sql.INVALID && e.msg == "a SELECT names no column; every_column() names every one"

    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [parameter(.integer(v: 1))])
    q.from @ []
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must() == Statement(text: "SELECT ?1", params: [.integer(v: 1)])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: select(from: "città", columns: [column("è")])), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"è\" FROM \"città\""

test "equal and not_equal: NULL written IS NULL and IS NOT NULL, with no parameter"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [equal("left_at", .null_value), not_equal("joined_at", .null_value)]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"left_at\" IS NULL AND \"joined_at\" IS NOT NULL"
    assert s.params == []
    q.where @ [compare(left: parameter(.null_value), op: .equal, right: column("a"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"a\" IS NULL"

test "less, less_or_equal, greater, greater_or_equal, between: a NULL refused, the largest and smallest i64"
    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [column("id")])

    for c in [less("a", .null_value), less_or_equal("a", .null_value), greater("a", .null_value), greater_or_equal("a", .null_value), between("a", low: .null_value, high: .integer(v: 1))]
        q.where @ [c]

        match to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres)
            .ok _  => assert false
            .err e => assert e.code == sql.INVALID

    q.where @ [between("a", low: .integer(v: -9223372036854775808), high: .integer(v: 9223372036854775807))]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE \"a\" BETWEEN ?1 AND ?2"
    assert s.params == [.integer(v: -9223372036854775808), .integer(v: 9223372036854775807)]
    q.where @ [
        less("a", .integer(v: 1))
        less_or_equal("b", .integer(v: 2))
        greater("c", .integer(v: 3))
        greater_or_equal("d", .integer(v: 4))
    ]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE \"a\" < $1 AND \"b\" <= $2 AND \"c\" > $3 AND \"d\" >= $4"

test "like and escape_like: empty, every special character, a text of more than one byte"
    assert escape_like("") == ""
    assert escape_like("100%_a\\b") == "100\\%\\_a\\\\b"
    assert escape_like("città%") == "città\\%"
    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [like(name: "name", pattern: "")]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE \"name\" LIKE $1 ESCAPE $2" && s.params == [.text(v: ""), .text(v: "\\")]

test "in_values: none, one, a NULL refused"
    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [in_values("id", values: [])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must() == Statement(text: "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE 1 = 0", params: [])
    q.where @ [negate(in_values("id", values: []))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE NOT (1 = 0)"
    q.where @ [in_values("id", values: [.text(v: "é")])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE \"id\" IN (?1)"
    q.where @ [in_values("id", values: [.integer(v: 1), .null_value])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).is_err()

test "all_of, any_of, negate, every_row: none, one, nested"
    a = equal("a", .integer(v: 1))
    b = equal("b", .integer(v: 2))
    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [all_of([])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE 1 = 1"
    q.where @ [any_of([])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE 1 = 0"
    q.where @ [all_of([a]), any_of([a, b]), every_row()]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE \"a\" = $1 AND (\"a\" = $2 OR \"b\" = $3) AND 1 = 1"
    q.where @ [negate(negate(a))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE NOT (NOT (\"a\" = ?1))"

test "compare and operation: other operators, a question mark for SQLite, an alias where none may stand"
    q @= select(from: "docs", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [compare(left: column("body"), op: .other(operator: "?"), right: parameter(.text(v: "k")))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"docs\" WHERE \"body\" ? $1"

    match to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == sql.UNSUPPORTED

    q.where @ [compare(left: column("a"), op: .other(operator: "= 1 --"), right: column("b"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).is_err()
    q.where @ [compare(left: aliased(column("a"), alias: "x"), op: .equal, right: column("b"))]

    match to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.msg == "an expression with another name (AS) stands only among the columns of a SELECT or of a RETURNING"

    q.where @ [
        compare(left: operation(left: column("a"), operator: "+", right: column("b")), op: .like, right: parameter(.text(v: "x")))
    ]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"docs\" WHERE (\"a\" + \"b\") LIKE $1 ESCAPE $2"

test "raw_without_values: each refusal, and a fragment as a condition"
    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [column("id")])

    for text in ["", "name = 'x'", "1; DROP TABLE t", "a -- b", "a /* b", "a = ?", "a = $1"]
        q.where @ [holds(raw_without_values(text))]

        match to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres)
            .ok _  => assert false
            .err e => assert e.code == sql.INVALID

    q.where @ [holds(raw_without_values("\"tsv\" @@ \"query\""))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"t\" WHERE (\"tsv\" @@ \"query\")"

test "call, call_distinct, cast, choose: a bad name, no argument, a bad type, no WHEN"
    for e in [call("lower(x)", arguments: []), call_distinct("count", arguments: []), cast(column("a"), type_name: "int; DROP"), choose([], otherwise: column("a"))]
        match to_sql(.select(query: select(from: "t", columns: [e])), .postgres)
            .ok _  => assert false
            .err x => assert x.code == sql.INVALID

test "limit and offset: zero, negative, an offset alone for each database"
    q @= select(from: "t", columns: [every_column()])
    q.limit @ ok(0)
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must() == Statement(text: "SELECT * FROM \"t\" LIMIT $1", params: [.integer(v: 0)])
    q.limit @ fail(code: sql.NOT_FOUND, msg: "no limit")
    q.offset @ ok(5)
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT * FROM \"t\" OFFSET $1"
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT * FROM \"t\" LIMIT -1 OFFSET ?1"
    q.offset @ ok(-1)
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).is_err()

test "combined: UNION ALL with the first's order, a combined SELECT's own limit refused, INTERSECT ALL for SQLite"
    q @= select(from: "a", columns: [column("id")])
    q.combined @ [Combined(operation: .union_all, query: select(from: "b", columns: [column("id")]))]
    q.order_by @ [ascending("id")]
    q.limit @ ok(2)
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"a\" UNION ALL SELECT \"id\" FROM \"b\" ORDER BY \"id\" LIMIT ?1"
    late @= select(from: "b", columns: [column("id")])
    late.limit @ ok(1)
    q.combined @ [Combined(operation: .union_distinct, query: late)]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).is_err()
    q.combined @ [Combined(operation: .intersect_all, query: select(from: "b", columns: [column("id")]))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"a\" INTERSECT ALL SELECT \"id\" FROM \"b\" ORDER BY \"id\" LIMIT $1"

    match to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == sql.UNSUPPORTED && e.msg == "INTERSECT ALL is PostgreSQL's alone, and SQLite has no form for it"

test "joins: each kind, a join with no condition, a cross join with one, nulls placed"
    q @= select(from: "a", columns: [every_column()])
    q.joins @ [
        left_join("b", on: columns_equal(left: "a.id", right: "b.a_id"))
        right_join("c", on: every_row())
        full_join("d", on: every_row())
        cross_join("e")
    ]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT * FROM \"a\" LEFT JOIN \"b\" ON \"a\".\"id\" = \"b\".\"a_id\" RIGHT JOIN \"c\" ON 1 = 1 FULL JOIN \"d\" ON 1 = 1 CROSS JOIN \"e\""
    q.joins @ [Join(kind: .inner, table: table("b"), on: [])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).is_err()
    q.joins @ [Join(kind: .cross, table: table("b"), on: [every_row()])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).is_err()
    q.joins @ []
    q.order_by @ [
        Order(by: column("a"), descending: false, nulls: .first)
        Order(by: column("b"), descending: true, nulls: .last)
    ]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT * FROM \"a\" ORDER BY \"a\" NULLS FIRST, \"b\" DESC NULLS LAST"

test "insert: none, a row of the wrong length, defaults, a conflict each way, from a SELECT with a conflict"
    match to_sql(.insert(query: insert(into: "t", columns: ["a"], rows: [])), .postgres)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.msg == "an INSERT has no row to insert"

    match to_sql(.insert(query: insert(into: "t", columns: ["a", "b"], rows: [[.integer(v: 1), .integer(v: 2)], [.integer(v: 3)]])), .postgres)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.msg == "row 1 of the INSERT holds 1 values for 2 columns"

    d @= insert(into: "t", columns: [], rows: [])
    d.source @ .defaults
    d.returning @ [column("id")]
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: d), .sqlite).must().text == "INSERT INTO \"t\" DEFAULT VALUES RETURNING \"id\""
    i @= insert(into: "t", columns: ["id", "name"], rows: [[.integer(v: 1), .text(v: "a")]])
    i.conflict @ .do_nothing(target: ["id"])
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: i), .postgres).must().text == "INSERT INTO \"t\" (\"id\", \"name\") VALUES ($1, $2) ON CONFLICT (\"id\") DO NOTHING"
    i.conflict @ .do_update(target: [], set: [assign("name", .text(v: "b"))])
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: i), .postgres).is_err()
    i.conflict @ .do_update(target: ["id"], set: [])
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: i), .postgres).is_err()
    from_users @= insert_select(into: "archive", columns: ["id"], query: select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")]))
    from_users.conflict @ .do_nothing(target: [])
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: from_users), .sqlite).must().text == "INSERT INTO \"archive\" (\"id\") SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE 1 = 1 ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING"

test "update and delete: no condition refused, no change refused, a dotted column refused"
    u @= update("users", set: [assign("name", .text(v: "z"))])

    match to_sql(.update(query: u), .postgres)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == sql.INVALID && e.msg == "the UPDATE of \"users\" has no condition, so it would change every row; every_row() in its where says that is meant"

    u.where @ [every_row()]
    assert to_sql(.update(query: u), .postgres).must().text == "UPDATE \"users\" SET \"name\" = $1 WHERE 1 = 1"
    u.set @ []
    assert to_sql(.update(query: u), .postgres).is_err()
    u.set @ [assign("users.name", .text(v: "z"))]
    assert to_sql(.update(query: u), .postgres).is_err()
    assert to_sql(.delete(query: delete("users")), .sqlite).is_err()

test "to_sql: the same bytes twice, and parameters numbered across subqueries in order"
    inner @= select(from: "posts", columns: [column("user_id")])
    inner.where @ [equal("title", .text(v: "b"))]
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [equal("name", .text(v: "a")), in_query("id", query: inner), equal("city", .text(v: "c"))]
    first = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert first == to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert first.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"name\" = $1 AND \"id\" IN (SELECT \"user_id\" FROM \"posts\" WHERE \"title\" = $2) AND \"city\" = $3"
    assert first.params == [.text(v: "a"), .text(v: "b"), .text(v: "c")]

## Examples

# A column named with its table.
test "column: a column of a named table"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [column("users.name")])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"users\".\"name\" FROM \"users\""

# A record's field names as a SELECT's columns, and a row read back into
# the record by the same names: `sql.Column::name` is the text `"name"`, and
# a field misspelt is a compile error on both sides. A program writes
# `user_of(row: sql.Row) -> User?` so, with `?` after each read.
test "columns_named: a record's fields as the columns, and its row read back by them"
    q = select(from: "catalog", columns: columns_named([sql.Column::name, sql.Column::type_name]))
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"name\", \"type_name\" FROM \"catalog\""
    row = sql.Row(names: ["name", "type_name"], values: [.text(v: "id"), .text(v: "INTEGER")])
    read = sql.Column(
        name: sql.text_named(row, sql.Column::name).must(),
        type_name: sql.text_named(row, sql.Column::type_name).must()
    )
    assert read == sql.Column(name: "id", type_name: "INTEGER")

# Every column of the table read.
test "every_column: SELECT *"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [every_column()])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT * FROM \"users\""

# Every column of one table of a join.
test "every_column_of: the columns of one table"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [every_column_of("users")])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"users\".* FROM \"users\""

# A value compared with a column travels as a parameter.
test "parameter: a value beside the text"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [compare(left: column("age"), op: .greater, right: parameter(.integer(v: 18)))]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" > $1" && s.params == [.integer(v: 18)]

# A function of SQL called on a column.
test "call: lower of a column"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [call("lower", arguments: [column("name")])])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT lower(\"name\") FROM \"users\""

# The count of the cities, each counted once.
test "call_distinct: count of distinct values"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [call_distinct("count", arguments: [column("city")])])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT count(DISTINCT \"city\") FROM \"users\""

# The rows of a table counted.
test "count_all: count(*)"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [count_all()])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT count(*) FROM \"users\""

# A text parameter read as PostgreSQL's `uuid`.
test "cast: a parameter as a uuid"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.where @ [
        compare(left: column("id"), op: .equal, right: cast(parameter(.text(v: "8f14e45f-ceea-467a-9c4e-0f3a8e6b6a1d")), type_name: "uuid"))
    ]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"id\" = CAST($1 AS uuid)"

# A price times a quantity.
test "operation: a product of two columns"
    q = select(from: "lines", columns: [operation(left: column("price"), operator: "*", right: column("quantity"))])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"price\" * \"quantity\" FROM \"lines\""

# A count under the name a record's field reads it by.
test "aliased: count(*) AS total"
    q = select(from: "users", columns: [aliased(count_all(), alias: "total")])
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT count(*) AS \"total\" FROM \"users\""

# A counter inserted, or raised by the hits proposed where its name is
# there already.
test "excluded: an upsert adding to a counter"
    i @= insert(into: "counters", columns: ["name", "hits"], rows: [[.text(v: "home"), .integer(v: 1)]])
    hits = operation(left: column("counters.hits"), operator: "+", right: excluded("hits"))
    i.conflict @ .do_update(target: ["name"], set: [Assignment(column: "hits", value: hits)])
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: i), .postgres).must().text == "INSERT INTO \"counters\" (\"name\", \"hits\") VALUES ($1, $2) ON CONFLICT (\"name\") DO UPDATE SET \"hits\" = \"counters\".\"hits\" + excluded.\"hits\""

# The users older than the average, a SELECT inside a condition.
test "subquery: a SELECT as a value"
    average = select(from: "users", columns: [call("avg", arguments: [column("age")])])
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.where @ [compare(left: column("age"), op: .greater, right: subquery(average))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" > (SELECT avg(\"age\") FROM \"users\")"

# A word chosen by a condition.
test "choose: CASE WHEN"
    kind = choose(
        [When(condition: greater("age", .integer(v: 17)), result: parameter(.text(v: "adult")))],
        otherwise: parameter(.text(v: "minor"))
    )
    s = to_sql(.select(query: select(from: "users", columns: [kind])), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT CASE WHEN \"age\" > $1 THEN $2 ELSE $3 END FROM \"users\""
    assert s.params == [.integer(v: 17), .text(v: "adult"), .text(v: "minor")]

# A word of SQL the package does not offer, holding no value.
test "raw_without_values: the current date"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.where @ [compare(left: column("born"), op: .less, right: raw_without_values("CURRENT_DATE"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"born\" < (CURRENT_DATE)"

# The users of one name.
test "equal: a column equal to a value"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [equal("name", .text(v: "Ziggy"))]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"name\" = $1" && s.params == [.text(v: "Ziggy")]

# The users who left, a NULL told by IS NOT NULL.
test "not_equal: a column that is not NULL"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [not_equal("left_at", .null_value)]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must() == Statement(text: "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"left_at\" IS NOT NULL", params: [])

# The users under eighteen.
test "less: a column below a value"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [less("age", .integer(v: 18))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" < $1"

# The users of eighteen or under.
test "less_or_equal: a column at most a value"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [less_or_equal("age", .integer(v: 18))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" <= ?1"

# The users over sixty-five.
test "greater: a column above a value"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [greater("age", .integer(v: 65))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" > $1"

# The users of eighteen or over.
test "greater_or_equal: a column at least a value"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [greater_or_equal("age", .integer(v: 18))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" >= $1"

# The names that begin with "Zig", the escape a parameter beside the
# pattern.
test "like: names beginning with a text"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [like(name: "name", pattern: "Zig%")]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"name\" LIKE $1 ESCAPE $2"
    assert s.params == [.text(v: "Zig%"), .text(v: "\\")]

# A text a person typed, matched as itself at the start of a column.
test "escape_like: a percent sign matched as itself"
    assert escape_like("50%") + "%" == "50\\%%"

# The users of three ids.
test "in_values: a column among values"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.where @ [in_values("id", values: [.integer(v: 1), .integer(v: 2), .integer(v: 3)])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"id\" IN ($1, $2, $3)"

# The users from eighteen to sixty-five.
test "between: a column within two values"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [between("age", low: .integer(v: 18), high: .integer(v: 65))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"age\" BETWEEN $1 AND $2"

# Each user's posts, joined where the ids meet.
test "columns_equal: a join's condition"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: columns_named(["users.name", "posts.title"]))
    q.joins @ [inner_join("posts", on: columns_equal(left: "users.id", right: "posts.user_id"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"users\".\"name\", \"posts\".\"title\" FROM \"users\" INNER JOIN \"posts\" ON \"users\".\"id\" = \"posts\".\"user_id\""

# An e-mail address compared without its case.
test "compare: an expression equal to a value"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [
        compare(left: call("lower", arguments: [column("email")]), op: .equal, right: parameter(.text(v: "z@example.org")))
    ]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE lower(\"email\") = $1"

# The users who wrote a post.
test "in_query: a column among a SELECT's rows"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.where @ [in_query("id", query: select(from: "posts", columns: [column("user_id")]))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"id\" IN (SELECT \"user_id\" FROM \"posts\")"

# The users who wrote a post, asked as whether one exists.
test "exists: a SELECT that answers a row"
    posts @= select(from: "posts", columns: [column("id")])
    posts.where @ [columns_equal(left: "posts.user_id", right: "users.id")]
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.where @ [exists(posts)]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE EXISTS (SELECT \"id\" FROM \"posts\" WHERE \"posts\".\"user_id\" = \"users\".\"id\")"

# Adults who are active, or any admin.
test "all_of: conditions that all hold, inside one of two"
    adult_and_active = all_of([greater_or_equal("age", .integer(v: 18)), equal("active", .boolean(v: true))])
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [any_of([adult_and_active, equal("admin", .boolean(v: true))])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE (\"age\" >= $1 AND \"active\" = $2) OR \"admin\" = $3"

# The users of one city or another.
test "any_of: one of two conditions"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [any_of([equal("city", .text(v: "London")), equal("city", .text(v: "Berlin"))])]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"city\" = $1 OR \"city\" = $2"

# The users of no listed id.
test "negate: NOT IN"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [negate(in_values("id", values: [.integer(v: 1), .integer(v: 2)]))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE NOT (\"id\" IN ($1, $2))"

# The users whose column of booleans is true.
test "holds: a column of booleans as the condition"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [holds(column("active"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"active\""

# Every session deleted, said on purpose.
test "every_row: a DELETE of every row"
    d @= delete("sessions")
    d.where @ [every_row()]
    assert to_sql(.delete(query: d), .postgres).must().text == "DELETE FROM \"sessions\" WHERE 1 = 1"

# A table in a schema.
test "table: a table with its schema"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.from @ [table("app.users")]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"app\".\"users\""

# A table read under a short name.
test "table_as: a table under another name"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("u.name")])
    q.from @ [table_as(name: "users", alias: "u")]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"u\".\"name\" FROM \"users\" AS \"u\""

# The ten newest posts read as a table.
test "from_subquery: a SELECT read as a table"
    recent @= select(from: "posts", columns: [column("id")])
    recent.order_by @ [descending("id")]
    recent.limit @ ok(10)
    q @= select(from: "posts", columns: [column("recent.id")])
    q.from @ [from_subquery(recent, alias: "recent")]
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"recent\".\"id\" FROM (SELECT \"id\" FROM \"posts\" ORDER BY \"id\" DESC LIMIT $1) AS \"recent\""

# The users with at least one post.
test "inner_join: the rows that match"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("users.name")])
    q.joins @ [inner_join("posts", on: columns_equal(left: "users.id", right: "posts.user_id"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"users\".\"name\" FROM \"users\" INNER JOIN \"posts\" ON \"users\".\"id\" = \"posts\".\"user_id\""

# Every user, with their posts where they have some.
test "left_join: every row before it kept"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: columns_named(["users.name", "posts.title"]))
    q.joins @ [left_join("posts", on: columns_equal(left: "users.id", right: "posts.user_id"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"users\".\"name\", \"posts\".\"title\" FROM \"users\" LEFT JOIN \"posts\" ON \"users\".\"id\" = \"posts\".\"user_id\""

# Every post, with its user where there is one.
test "right_join: every row of the joined table kept"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("posts.title")])
    q.joins @ [right_join("posts", on: columns_equal(left: "users.id", right: "posts.user_id"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"posts\".\"title\" FROM \"users\" RIGHT JOIN \"posts\" ON \"users\".\"id\" = \"posts\".\"user_id\""

# Every user and every post, matched where they meet.
test "full_join: every row of both sides kept"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [every_column()])
    q.joins @ [full_join("posts", on: columns_equal(left: "users.id", right: "posts.user_id"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT * FROM \"users\" FULL JOIN \"posts\" ON \"users\".\"id\" = \"posts\".\"user_id\""

# Every size with every colour.
test "cross_join: every row with every row"
    q @= select(from: "sizes", columns: columns_named(["sizes.name", "colours.name"]))
    q.joins @ [cross_join("colours")]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"sizes\".\"name\", \"colours\".\"name\" FROM \"sizes\" CROSS JOIN \"colours\""

# The users by name, from A.
test "ascending: ORDER BY a column"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.order_by @ [ascending("name")]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" ORDER BY \"name\""

# The oldest users first.
test "descending: ORDER BY a column DESC"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("name")])
    q.order_by @ [descending("age")]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"name\" FROM \"users\" ORDER BY \"age\" DESC"

# The first page of the active users by name.
test "select: a page of rows"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: columns_named(["id", "name"]))
    q.where @ [equal("active", .boolean(v: true))]
    q.order_by @ [ascending("name")]
    q.limit @ ok(10)
    s = to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "SELECT \"id\", \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"active\" = $1 ORDER BY \"name\" LIMIT $2"
    assert s.params == [.boolean(v: true), .integer(v: 10)]

# A user renamed.
test "assign: a column given a value"
    u @= update("users", set: [assign("name", .text(v: "Aladdin"))])
    u.where @ [equal("id", .integer(v: 7))]
    s = to_sql(.update(query: u), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "UPDATE \"users\" SET \"name\" = $1 WHERE \"id\" = $2" && s.params == [.text(v: "Aladdin"), .integer(v: 7)]

# Two users inserted, their new ids answered.
test "insert: two rows, RETURNING"
    i @= insert(
        into: "users",
        columns: ["name", "email"],
        rows: [[.text(v: "Ziggy"), .text(v: "z@example.org")], [.text(v: "Aladdin"), .text(v: "a@example.org")]]
    )
    i.returning @ [column("id")]
    s = to_sql(.insert(query: i), .postgres).must()
    assert s.text == "INSERT INTO \"users\" (\"name\", \"email\") VALUES ($1, $2), ($3, $4) RETURNING \"id\""
    assert s.params.len() == 4

# The inactive users copied into an archive.
test "insert_select: rows from a SELECT"
    inactive @= select(from: "users", columns: columns_named(["id", "name"]))
    inactive.where @ [equal("active", .boolean(v: false))]
    i = insert_select(into: "archive", columns: ["id", "name"], query: inactive)
    assert to_sql(.insert(query: i), .sqlite).must().text == "INSERT INTO \"archive\" (\"id\", \"name\") SELECT \"id\", \"name\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"active\" = ?1"

# The users not seen since a moment made inactive, their ids answered.
test "update: rows changed, RETURNING"
    u @= update("users", set: [assign("active", .boolean(v: false))])
    u.where @ [less("seen", .integer(v: 1700000000))]
    u.returning @ [column("id")]
    assert to_sql(.update(query: u), .postgres).must().text == "UPDATE \"users\" SET \"active\" = $1 WHERE \"seen\" < $2 RETURNING \"id\""

# One user deleted.
test "delete: one row"
    d @= delete("users")
    d.where @ [equal("id", .integer(v: 7))]
    assert to_sql(.delete(query: d), .sqlite).must() == Statement(text: "DELETE FROM \"users\" WHERE \"id\" = ?1", params: [.integer(v: 7)])

# One query written for each database: only the placeholders differ.
test "to_sql: the same query for PostgreSQL and for SQLite"
    q @= select(from: "users", columns: [column("id")])
    q.where @ [equal("name", .text(v: "Ziggy"))]
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .postgres).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"name\" = $1"
    assert to_sql(.select(query: q), .sqlite).must().text == "SELECT \"id\" FROM \"users\" WHERE \"name\" = ?1"
